Its range is $y\\in [-2,2]$\n- $y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is an output of the function" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. At $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, the function $y=cos⁡x$ takes the value zero.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. The range of the function $y=cos⁡x$ is $y\\in [-1,1].$ Thus, $y=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is not an output of this function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13cf2350-5cb8-5bce-82d7-7d69855b39b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235935} {:answer/id #uuid "3656391b-7c11-597f-a2bd-8b6a0f95faaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236162} {:answer/id #uuid "431b8b2c-60f9-50ed-a73e-335785cc18ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236250} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c131e4-70f4-5b57-9ffb-981148d71879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236948} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d7012c-5356-5c36-b422-e156f9cbdec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2c131e4-70f4-5b57-9ffb-981148d71879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236948} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b37897-7ab2-5034-96e3-b9f2f4798c3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4afb86c8-970f-594e-bb88-36f6e2c3e725" :question/math-academy-id "q-103233" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The zeros of the function are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi $\n- Its graph passes through the point $(0,1)$\n- The function has maximum values at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The zeros occur at $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…,$ which can be written as $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is true. At $x=0,$ the function has the value $y=cos⁡0=1.$ Therefore, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a maximum at $x=0,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=2n\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2f2bf1-9489-53d0-aef7-7228b981bac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235998} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff2b5ac-aa6d-5187-84fd-8accf825947a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236023} {:answer/id #uuid "4126e729-233b-53a1-875e-c58b84df84ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236232} {:answer/id #uuid "75f6292f-0f0c-5b71-9614-126a4b2d68e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236556} {:answer/id #uuid "83034a3e-d469-513a-b703-047d7a3470a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c2f2bf1-9489-53d0-aef7-7228b981bac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235998} :answer-field/id #uuid "92061e84-03fe-54ba-9008-b9fd45a3987b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2215b590-8140-5323-92bf-8ae24e1b7bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-103289" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nI. The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $\n\nII. The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n$\n\nIII. The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/73/732955302ca49a41f6c43cf242d7c869e630e88d804c4b56a5beb3b081f2d230.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is defined everywhere, except where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32d059bf-52c4-58a9-9b0a-80f98273a3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236136} {:answer/id #uuid "60ffe8f6-7519-5c83-8e3b-7933d314d384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236435} {:answer/id #uuid "760810ba-4dba-5546-b9e0-25979b61215c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236557} {:answer/id #uuid "c375d3e0-e998-53aa-9b1a-df787d700c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237052} {:answer/id #uuid "de60a5ae-237a-5bdb-ad3c-19e44d3dcfba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "760810ba-4dba-5546-b9e0-25979b61215c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236557} :answer-field/id #uuid "137c3137-fea5-56a8-8a43-b7a5f0e8b08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f5925ee-40c3-50af-9950-ecf1ea188724" :question/math-academy-id "q-103295" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\n- The vertical asymptotes of the function are $x=2n\\pi $\n- Its graph passes through the point $(0,0)$\n- $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2d0606829588b01f6cba2fa4785b201c47a6d9ffa325d54e64544c9769d7e4a.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has an asymptote at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,0).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d36593-75d4-519d-80e3-889e4ae17035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235830} {:answer/id #uuid "769024b7-4204-5e89-bc5a-680d454811d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236560} {:answer/id #uuid "b71e7e30-fa35-5b8a-b30b-27e146881fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236979} {:answer/id #uuid "f18fe270-14b9-57b0-badb-0ac47a2b4d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237339} {:answer/id #uuid "f47bb03f-7102-529c-83bb-ebc037b3713d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f18fe270-14b9-57b0-badb-0ac47a2b4d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237339} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f8736d1-5cde-5191-9d87-b4dcebf146f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55643e28-3c44-53e1-a6da-d559974acbda" :question/math-academy-id "q-103811" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\n- The domain of the function is all positive real numbers.\n- $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, the statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi .$ So, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6\\pi ).$ Hence, statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161c32fd-bb0c-5c3a-9a70-2e38ff080ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235952} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9c0b4a-86ce-59cd-bea8-fa20dd7847c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236003} {:answer/id #uuid "786030d4-25c9-5b36-b415-912b55a11f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236572} {:answer/id #uuid "aeae4205-e6c3-5d0f-9138-98e42125bc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236929} {:answer/id #uuid "b43c6310-9354-5206-886d-6239c7a82269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c9c0b4a-86ce-59cd-bea8-fa20dd7847c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236003} :answer-field/id #uuid "cace984a-b193-5b87-8192-dfb23054b530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "048ceaff-1e4b-5807-8d50-fab96c1502f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103814" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\n- $y=1$ is the minimum value of the function. $$\n- $x=9\\pi $ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n- The function takes the value $1$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/06/066fe6ce8a65927436d64753ec4e61bfea257405e4b2f81554ac8341572bcf15.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )$, therefore the function doesn't have a minimum value. Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi $ for any integer $n$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence statement II is true.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .$ Hence statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97842440-8562-5084-8515-ac9bed4f30cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239366} {:answer/id #uuid "adf67485-98f6-5532-b74e-ad997abb4115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239564} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f0ae0b-0e27-5e62-ba20-05fe58021513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239664} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1ddd11-773f-5bba-9c4f-50345066fd1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239859} {:answer/id #uuid "edb1bc67-aa4a-5b31-9afb-59791c7d655e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edb1bc67-aa4a-5b31-9afb-59791c7d655e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240149} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b0cf691-b10b-512c-8716-cad8675c07cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "340a8e11-ca0d-5e08-ab08-b9647c00e4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-104049" :question/problem "![](images/51/5143df65d0f99c6511dc8ab13262831669c88535e19c986dacdb364e04847f58.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=2$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8bd6dd-1931-5df3-803b-dc0e5167142e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238463} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ed7c5-b2af-580b-8113-c798acb35065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238721} {:answer/id #uuid "92b58f29-e9d2-52ea-80a1-a85417e0693d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239321} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b9cbc0-4187-50a5-81fd-c6539f6fe9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239932} {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c90896-bfe8-504a-9692-0915b93a4eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104051" :question/problem "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013b8848-3885-5ddc-abba-c6d98f5249e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238091} {:answer/id #uuid "60b0fbff-7602-5867-b0a7-49ff7cf237ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238904} {:answer/id #uuid "ac75e25e-87f7-560b-989e-a0db688ca52b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239554} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fdba29-79ec-5716-8a2d-1c3ed48f88b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239755} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb22f50-3d68-5e9a-9196-af09faba8f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60b0fbff-7602-5867-b0a7-49ff7cf237ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238904} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e6d376-017a-5f97-89b1-398a2e796dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3312f9fe-c2a1-5d75-b752-6c981a0a3f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104052" :question/problem "![](images/80/8086893e4587e68f8257b75f2070cf622fa141199d6bd8960f7c4fd8e7bcb349.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d0261a4-d877-5906-ad0b-830747a5cc4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239135} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad4d9e3-4681-5c2a-91a3-0ffe435a5b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239261} {:answer/id #uuid "b164497e-772a-5260-941e-feac495b0767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239604} {:answer/id #uuid "bb707977-2299-58a7-be64-009156d71aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239688} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c3ed1b-b297-5c48-93a5-4e4568f8c09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb707977-2299-58a7-be64-009156d71aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239688} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0db112b-2842-5c5e-b64c-f115b941ce9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad98348d-cbe6-535a-9828-f9008c2ec2e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104124" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdcfa35fb04028cbff32992b41214c878443d3815b676a0cf046f5a99bd771bd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=-2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=f(-2)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x) & =5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=5=f(-2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f11833-470e-5bef-bed8-6e5dd7f35fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238821} {:answer/id #uuid "73bfaf49-d235-5587-b751-402d142ac763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239046} {:answer/id #uuid "a836c130-129a-569d-9e28-b1933339a9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239516} {:answer/id #uuid "b09885ad-d9c7-552d-b4c7-930d5c59c1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239589} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3240e0-b339-53b6-a701-9e9f4809ca9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b09885ad-d9c7-552d-b4c7-930d5c59c1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239589} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fa88a49-b814-5ee5-80f8-03e880fa2400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ff94cca-f0c0-5b4f-9e96-5ccdc57e1837" :question/math-academy-id "q-104573" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=(2x-y)\\,i-x\\,j$\n- $G=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-y$ and $Q=-x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q=-{e}^{x-y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c39dccc-d152-5823-8cfe-96d61199aa56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238165} {:answer/id #uuid "21e993fb-7e01-5a59-b005-ac176de2681e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238349} {:answer/id #uuid "2643658b-7491-52bd-8be9-74fb74f6311b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238387} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0c18d7-5599-5d7e-8192-2ab68f7c76f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239392} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba2a58b-9f8a-5330-9034-f99d18182f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e993fb-7e01-5a59-b005-ac176de2681e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238349} :answer-field/id #uuid "97fa4ed2-4752-5f83-95ae-700c3f3d2fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aac0da30-e1e4-5293-838c-ddb161ffe326" :question/math-academy-id "q-104582" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative in their respective domains?\n\n- $F=(2x-4y)\\,i+(4x-8y)\\,j$\n- $G={2}^{2x+y+1}\\,i+{2}^{2x+y}\\,j$\n- $H=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-4y$ and $Q=4x-8y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={2}^{2x+y+1}$ and $Q={2}^{2x+y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$ and $Q=\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005c8e0e-15a2-595a-8bf4-9ff48deefe1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238082} {:answer/id #uuid "5a77fe81-e649-5e06-8e48-0a04dd7aa841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238835} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ee5d37-f557-5170-896e-a0186821d84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239673} {:answer/id #uuid "ed516192-424f-5a7a-a664-5896e5376a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240142} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e231ae-d734-5b35-81c3-81ff9183634d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed516192-424f-5a7a-a664-5896e5376a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240142} :answer-field/id #uuid "86eda225-7e4d-5e5f-9ce8-78deff9e5db6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b5e7ba9-0b08-5996-9edf-60b5549da2af" :question/math-academy-id "q-105046" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0bc0e75bb577ca37b89568e14a4e0d03da7ce4f4853c4e8c57d9b2df79897b13.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a92cc1-113b-5b68-8eee-53888758e96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238139} {:answer/id #uuid "45f98056-476a-56c3-a387-1679af854fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238663} {:answer/id #uuid "e226facb-7893-5854-8a55-7e51661183d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240043} {:answer/id #uuid "ef23eeaa-e349-5747-9c7d-4d499cf8c5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240164} {:answer/id #uuid "fac16b79-d230-552c-b2c9-9227e7ed3b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef23eeaa-e349-5747-9c7d-4d499cf8c5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240164} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb9b362c-341e-588a-98f7-56e59732034e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c30bad2f-8979-5b57-acae-23ae1c82d606" :question/math-academy-id "q-105573" :question/problem "![](images/30/3088d088689bbd0dfd33abe8240c6f02cceb257937fc1fccb8ba3df151dbfaab.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "627918cc-4b21-5f54-b208-cb86d8a90fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238922} {:answer/id #uuid "9f41d10b-f851-5d45-9827-61f7b022fc29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239445} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e51c7e-db29-5f2f-8366-bcfa0cafd1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239898} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb49dbb-7200-53b3-81d0-14d6d6fcc22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239981} {:answer/id #uuid "e21e0841-2d32-541f-bd4e-00d6bca599f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/